+### The Director does the spawning. You ask it to.
 
+**Changed 17 September 2026 (the Message Load mission, owner decision 2).** A restore used to be a
+list of `cc-devthrottle session spawn ... --controlled-by <the owner's id>` lines for the restoring
+session to run. The Gateway now refuses a session that names ANY other session as the owner of what it
+starts - that is the boundary that decides who may message whom - so those lines fail for every seat
+owned by somebody else, which is nearly every Worker. Do not look for a way round it. Ask the
+Director:
+
 ```bash
-cc-devthrottle session spawn "<repoPath>" \
-  --director "<the NEW director id>" \
-  --agent <the same agent> \
-  --name "<the original name>" \
-  --role <the original role> \
-  --mission <missionId, if it had one> \
-  --controlled-by <the restored manager's new id, or --standalone> \
-  --prompt "<seed>"
+cc-devthrottle director restore "<workspace id>" --director "<the NEW director id>"
 ```
 
-`--args` is passed through literally and applies no defaults, so if you set it to pin a model you
-must supply the permission preset too, or the session blocks on a prompt for every action.
+The Director reads the workspace, and for every seat decided `restore` that has not come back it:
+
+- starts the seat itself, on its own credential, through the same spawn route every client uses;
+- names the owner the seat had when the Gateway captured it - never one you supply;
+- starts an owner that was restarted in the same drain FIRST, and names it by its NEW id (the
+  placeholder `<the new id of ...>` the old lines carried is resolved for you, in order);
+- keeps the id of an owner on another Director, which survived the restart;
+- does NOT start a seat whose owner is not coming back (decided `close`, failed, or not yet restored),
+  and says so on that seat - it never starts it unowned or under a dead id;
+- writes each result onto its seat as it goes: `restoredSessionId` when it came back,
+  `restore.failure` with the reason when it did not, and `restore.attemptedAtUtc` either way. One seat
+  failing does not stop the rest;
+- never starts a seat that already came back, so asking twice is safe.
 
+The command waits (ten minutes by default, `--wait-seconds`) and prints one line per seat -
+`RESTORED`, `FAILED` with the reason, or `PENDING` if the wait ran out. It exits 0 only when every seat
+came back. The restore is refused before anything starts when the workspace was not captured from a
+Director, when nothing is left to bring back, when the Director is on a different machine from the
+one the workspace was captured on, or when a restore is already running there.
+
+**Your decisions are still yours - write them onto the workspace first.** A seat you decided not to
+restore (above) gets `"decision": "close"` on the workspace BEFORE you ask, or the Director will bring
+it back. To go one seat at a time - the head of a mission first, check it, then anything else - pass
+`--seat <captured session id>`, repeated as needed. The per-seat command the drain wrote into each
+record is exactly that form; replace `<the NEW director id>` before running it.
+
+The restore records the session that asked as each new seat's parent. It is never recorded as
+anybody's owner, and you do not need to own anything to ask.
+
+`--agent`, `--role`, `--mission` and the workflow seat come from the capture too. There is no way to
+pass `--args` through a restore; a seat comes back with the Director's default agent settings.
+
